{{also|persécution}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sekʷ-|id=follow}}
Equivalent to {{suffix|en|persecute|ion}}. From {{inh|en|enm|persecucioun}}, from {{der|en|fro|persecucion}},<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> from {{der|en|EL.|persecūtio||persecution; chase, pursuit}}, from {{der|en|la|persequor||follow up, pursue}}, from {{prefix|la|per|sequor|t1=through|t2=follow|nocat=1}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|ēhtnes}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌpɝsəˈkjuʃən/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌpɜːsəˈkjuːʃən/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-persecution.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|per|se|cu|tion}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of [[persecute|persecuting]], especially a  [[specific]] group of [[people]]; an instance of persecution.
#: {{ux|en|Many [[apartheid]] perpetrators escaped [[prosecution]] for their '''persecution''' of black Africans and political dissidents.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=Jan 1 2004|journal=BioScience|url=https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/54/1/50/234599|publisher=Oxford University Press|title=The Practices of Wolf '''Persecution''', Protection, and Restoration in Canada and the United States|passage=During the early 20th century, wolves were considered undesirable. They were subject to '''persecution''' and were extirpated from large areas of their original range. With increased environmental awareness in the 1970s, attitudes toward wolves began to change.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=w:Jan Sapp|title=Race Finished|volume=100|issue=2|page=164|magazine=w:American Scientist|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/race-finished|passage=Few concepts are as emotionally charged as that of race. The word conjures up a mixture of associations—culture, ethnicity, genetics, subjugation, exclusion and '''persecution'''. But is the tragic history of efforts to define groups of people by race really a matter of the misuse of science, the abuse of a valid biological concept?}}

